<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="chlorophyll cycle" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5068" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: CHLOROPHYLL-A| (Chl&lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt;) and |FRAME: CHLOROPHYLL-B| (Chl&lt;i&gt;b&lt;/i&gt;) are light harvesting pigments that function to capture photons and transfer them to the reaction centers. Chl&lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; and Chl&lt;i&gt;b&lt;/i&gt; share the same structure except for the side chain at C-7 which is a methyl group in Chl&lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; and a formyl group in Chl&lt;i&gt;b&lt;/i&gt;. Whereas the biosynthesis of Chl&lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; has been widely reported (see |FRAME:PWY-5086| and |FRAME:PWY-5064|), that of Chl&lt;i&gt;b&lt;/i&gt; has been more elusive. Although it has long been known that |FRAME: CHLOROPHYLL-A| appears before |FRAME: CHLOROPHYLL-B| when chlorophyll synthesis begins, little was known of the formation of the formyl group of Chl&lt;i&gt;b&lt;/i&gt;. The discovery of the |FRAME: AT1G44446-MONOMER| (CAO) showed that this enzyme was sufficient for the transition of &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt;- to &lt;i&gt;b&lt;/i&gt;-type chlorophyll pigments. The enzyme was originally discovered in |FRAME: TAX-3052|, where mutants lacking in |FRAME:CHLOROPHYLL-B| biosynthesis were generated, and the gene responsible for the phenotype was isolated |CITS: [9770552]|. Subsequent work showed that the homologous |FRAME:AT1G44446| gene from |FRAME: ORG-5993| is required for chlorophyll b synthesis in that organism |CITS:[10758481][10468639]|. The reduction of Chl&lt;i&gt;b&lt;/i&gt; back to Chl&lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt; (and its hydrolysis to chlorophyllide &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt;) is not as well characterized, although strongly supported by observations. The process has been demonstrated &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; in etioplasts and etioplast membranes preparations from several organisms |CITS: [8215395][8071325][8576141][8954166][9857045]|. The compound |FRAME: CPD-7016| was identified as an intermediate in the reduction reaction and it was found that the reduction of |FRAME: CHLOROPHYLL-B| to |FRAME: CPD-7016| proceeded with NADPH as reductant while the next step, reduction of |FRAME: CPD-7016| to |FRAME: CHLOROPHYLL-A|, required reduced ferredoxin |CITS: [9857045]|. This fact indicated that two enzymes, |FRAME: MONOMER-13499| and |FRAME: MONOMER-13500|, contribute to the overall reaction |CITS: [16228557]|. These combined findings suggest the existence of a cyclic pathway for the interconversion of the two types of chlorophyll molecules, as a response to changing light conditions |CITS:[8576141][16228557]|.</Description>
